ID: B3-Ch02b-Weird
TEXT:

"Were the buildings here always this crooked?" I glance up at the overhangs crowding the street. Three-story stone facades jut out over wooden foundations at angles that look ready to collapse under their own weight. "I'm surprised half the block hasn't slid into the gutter."

"A few were like this before they threw me out, but nothing like this mess," Melindra says, stepping over a cracked paving stone. "They tore down half the old quarter to stack new floors on top. It's Billy Bob's handiwork, almost certainly. The rogue lessathi I mentioned at the gate. He considers himself a universal genius—architecture included—and the king handed him free rein as a reward for dragging Ollendor into the current century overnight. So Billy Bob spent his time experimenting with every bizarre design that popped into his head. Some hold up. Others, not so much."

"Excuse me," Daren says, his brow furrowing as he looks over. "Did you say his name is Billy Bob?"

"Of course not," Melindra says cheerfully. "Nobody could pronounce his actual name without biting their tongue off. Biliborrathrathsarran. The first two syllables sound vaguely like Billy Bob, so it stuck."

"His parents must have had a grand sense of humor," Hadrik says with a grin.

"It's no worse than any other lessathi name," Melindra says. "Just longer. There are a dozen sensible ways to shorten it, which convinces me his parents saddled him with it out of pure spite. What better way to ruin a child's life than to name him—look, a flying golem head."

"What?" Hadrik blinks.

"Right there," Melindra points toward the sky ahead. "Coming straight for us."

The thing droning down the street is a brass sphere the size of a human skull, kept aloft by a spinning twin-blade propeller mounted on top. In its center sits a single glass lens, glowing with dull crimson light. As the contraption drops to eye level, the sphere tilts left and right, its aperture clicking as it scans each of us in turn.

A flat, grinding monotone issues from a grille beneath the lens. "This unit does not recognize you. Identify yourselves."

"Oh, the memories," Melindra says, folding her arms with a grin. "Billy Bob was obsessed with these one-eyed toys. Wanted to make them intelligent. Called them droons, if I remember right. He had this grand vision of filling the skies with them so they could police the citizens without paying city guards. Naturally, it never went anywhere. The best he managed was teaching the relics to demand a name. If you stay quiet, it buzzes around your ears all day. Give it any nonsense word you like, and it flies off satisfied. A triumph of modern engineering."

"This unit shall not repeat itself again," the brass sphere grinds out, hovering six feet in front of my face. "Identify yourselves, or face the consequences!"

choice(""I am Felran, the strongest fire elementalist on the Eastern Continent!"", B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_b3_ch2_name = 0)
choice(""My name is Cutthroat Dave."", B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_b3_ch2_name = 1)
choice(""My name is Barry."", B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_b3_ch2_name = 2)
choice("Stab the flying metal ball in the eye", B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_b3_ch2_name = 3)
choice("Tell everyone that we should just ignore the metal ball, and go on our way", B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Designation, v_b3_ch2_name = 4)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Designation
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_name == 0) {
"I am Felran, the strongest fire elementalist on the Eastern Continent!"

"Designation acquired," the brass sphere hums, the crimson lens pulsing once. "You will henceforth be referred to as Felran, the strongest fire elementalist on the Eastern Continent. Have a pleasant day."

The rotor whines as the sphere banks upward and drifts away over the rooftops.

"Wait, that's it?" Hadrik scratches his beard, staring after it. "It's not going to ask the rest of us?"

"I told you they aren't brilliant," Melindra says.

"Yeah, but still," Hadrik says. "Is the whole city going to call all of us Felran now, or what?"

"Possibly," Melindra says with a shrug. "No telling what modifications were made after I left. Doesn't matter either way. Let's move."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_name == 1) {
"My name is Cutthroat Dave."

"Designation acquired," the brass sphere hums, the crimson lens pulsing once. "You will henceforth be referred to as Cutthroat Dave. Have a pleasant day."

The rotor whines as the sphere banks upward and drifts away over the rooftops.

"Wait, that's it?" Hadrik scratches his beard, staring after it. "It's not going to ask the rest of us?"

"I told you they aren't brilliant," Melindra says.

"Yeah, but still," Hadrik says. "Is the whole city going to call all of us Cutthroat Dave now, or what?"

"Possibly," Melindra says with a shrug. "No telling what modifications were made after I left. Doesn't matter either way. Let's move."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_name == 2) {
"My name is Barry."

"Designation acquired," the brass sphere hums, the crimson lens pulsing once. "You will henceforth be referred to as Barry. Have a pleasant day."

The rotor whines as the sphere banks upward and drifts away over the rooftops.

"Wait, that's it?" Hadrik scratches his beard, staring after it. "It's not going to ask the rest of us?"

"I told you they aren't brilliant," Melindra says.

"Yeah, but still," Hadrik says. "Is the whole city going to call all of us Barry now, or what?"

"Possibly," Melindra says with a shrug. "No telling what modifications were made after I left. Doesn't matter either way. Let's move."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_name == 3) {
Instead of answering, I slide my dagger from my belt and drive the steel tip straight into the glass aperture. The sphere shudders violently, gears squealing in a shrill metallic rasp. The red light flickers out, the rotor coughs and seizes, and the brass ball drops into the dirt with a hollow clatter.

"Well," I say, nudging the dented shell with my boot. "I was half-expecting an alarm or an exploding ward."

"Told you," Melindra says, stepping around the scrap. "Useless contraptions. A waste of brass. Still, with that one wrecked, it won't be buzzing around our heads. Let's keep moving."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_name == 4) {
"I say we simply ignore it," I tell the others. "I see no reason why I should give this flying contraption my name."

"You can't ignore it," Melindra says. "It'll trail you from street to street until you feed it a word."

"Let's just head for the castle," I say, stepping past the hovering metal ball.

Melindra shrugs. "Suit yourself."

She takes the lead again, and the rest of us follow close behind.

"This is your last warning!" the metallic voice buzzes right behind my ear as the sphere matches our pace. "Identify yourself, or you will face the consequences."

"How many last warnings does this thing have?" I mutter over my shoulder. "Didn't it just claim it wouldn't repeat itself?"

"Empty threats," Melindra says without looking back. "It'll string last warnings together until sundown unless you answer. There's no getting around it."

"Refusing to identify yourself is a direct violation of the law," the sphere rattles, hovering closer. "State your name now, or you will be deemed a criminal!"

Around us, pedestrians begin slowing down. Heads turn, and several locals back toward the walls, eyeing us with open unease.

"We're drawing too much attention with this nuisance trailing behind," Daren mutters, then turns squarely toward the floating sphere. "Hey! Flying contraption! My name is Daren. Do you understand? Da-ren!"

"Designation acquired," the brass sphere hums, its red lens pulsing once. "You will henceforth be referred to as Da-ren. Have a pleasant day."

The rotor whines as the sphere banks upward and drifts away over the rooftops.

"Wait, that's it?" Hadrik scratches his beard, staring after it. "It's not going to ask the rest of us?"

"I told you they aren't brilliant," Melindra says.

"Yeah, but still," Hadrik says. "Is the whole city going to call all of us Daren now, or what?"

"Possibly," Melindra says with a shrug. "No telling what modifications were made after I left. Doesn't matter either way. Let's move."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Esteemed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Esteemed) if (v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3 || v_b3_ch2_goblins == 4 || v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3)
