}
"Hey, no problem!" I say, taking a step forward. "In that case, I guess we'll be goin--"

"In order for this unit to properly guide you through the city, you must first specify your intended destination," the sphere hums, hovering directly in my path.

"I just told you we don't need a guide," I say. "We know the way."

"Negative," the sphere replies. "All merchants arriving from foreign cities are our esteemed guests. It is this unit's primary directive to ensure your commerce proceeds as smoothly as possible. In order to guide you, however, this unit requires your destination."

"And what if we refuse to provide one?" Daren asks.

"Error," the sphere clicks, the green light stuttering to a dull amber before flashing bright white. "This unit is not equipped to resolve this scenario. Initiating experimental artificial intelligence protocol: designation Overseer. Please standby. Your inquiry will be addressed shortly."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Formulating, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Formulating)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Formulating
TEXT:

"This is ridiculous," Melindra says, waving a dismissive hand. "Why stand here listening to a brainless contraption rattle off nonsense? Leave the overgrown brass ball behind before it wastes another second of our--"

"Overseer protocol initiated," the sphere hums, the white light stabilizing into a deep, steady pulse. "Analyzing situational parameters. Please standby."

"Melindra has the right of it," Hadrik mutters, adjusting his axe strap. "We aren't getting anywhere standing in the road while--"

"Analysis complete," the sphere announces cheerfully. "Thank you for your patience. Current assessment: your party entered Ollendor illegally under the guise of foreign merchants, and you are currently advancing toward the royal palace. Is this correct?"

Hadrik stops in his tracks, his jaw dropping as he stares at the floating bronze orb. "Hold on. What did you just--"

"No, that's completely wrong!" I tell the sphere. "We didn't sneak in. We used the proper password at the front gate!"

"Objection logged," the sphere replies without hesitation. "Formulating revised hypothesis based on newly acquired parameters."

"Listen, we don't need guidance," I say, stepping around it. "Just go back to wherever you--"

"Revised hypothesis generated," the sphere cuts in. "Current assessment: entry through the city gates was procedurally valid, but the authorization password was obtained through unlawful coercion or theft. Is this correct?"

"No!" I snap. "Why would you assume that? We received the password legitimately from a local sponsor!"

"Based on all contextual and behavioral metrics," the sphere drones pleasantly, "the Overseer protocol concludes that your primary objective in Ollendor is the assassination of the king. Is this correct?"

choice(""No, that's not true at all!"", B3-Ch02b-Defective, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Defective, v_b3_ch2_confess = 0)
choice(""Fine, you got us! Are you happy now?"", B3-Ch02b-Defective, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Defective, v_b3_ch2_confess = 1)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Defective
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch2_lie,1) if (v_b3_ch2_confess == 1)
achievement("I cannot tell a lie.",v_ac_b3_ch2_lie)
#if(v_b3_ch2_confess == 0) {
"No, that's not true at all!"

"Processing..." The sphere clicks several times. "It appears the Overseer protocol has failed to provide relevant assistance. This protocol remains in an experimental phase and may occasionally generate inaccurate guidance. As this unit cannot fulfill your navigational requirements, you are advised to consult an officer of the city watch, who will gladly attend to your needs. We apologize for the inconvenience. Have a pleasant day!"

The propeller whines as the sphere banks sharply and bobs away over the rooftops.

"Well, damn," Hadrik mutters, watching it vanish into the sky. "I was braced for the blasted thing to start blaring an alarm across the district. Is there any chance that golem head runs off to report its 'analysis' to the garrison?"

"I don't know, but I don't like the look on these bystanders," Daren murmurs. He jerks his chin toward several townspeople who have stopped along the storefronts, watching us with tense, wary eyes. "No telling how much of that exchange carried down the street. We need to clear out before we draw any more attention."

"How do you suppose that scrap metal figured out our business anyway?" Hadrik asks Daren under his breath as we put distance between ourselves and the onlookers.

"They might have tracked us before we even reached the gates," Daren says. "Melindra said surveillance was the original intent, and I haven't been watching the sky since we crossed the threshold."

"If they knew our plans before we walked in, why let us stroll halfway to the castle?" Hadrik asks.

"A trap, perhaps," Daren replies. "Or their network is as broken as the rest of this machinery, and the reports are rotting in an unread dispatch log. Either way, we assume enemies around every turn. Melindra, how far to this plaza?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_confess == 1) {
"Fine, you got us! Are you happy now?"

"Query received and acknowledged," the sphere hums smoothly. "Please standby. The Overseer protocol will now assist your mission to the best of its operational parameters."

Hadrik blinks. "Wait, what?..."

"Calculating group probability of success for assassinating the king of Ollendor," the sphere rattles off. "Calculation complete. Your current odds of successfully completing this objective are exceedingly low. Would you like to review the operational analysis?"

"Uhm, sure," I say, glancing at the others. "Why not?"

"Given your party's estimated combat metrics, the most probable outcome is total defeat by Ollendor's royal guard prior to reaching the primary target," the sphere says. "However, a single viable scenario exists wherein your party could stall the defenders long enough to eliminate the king, though your subsequent survival rate remains negligible."

"And what scenario is that?" I ask.

"You must exploit the escalating friction between the veteran officers of the royal guard and the mercenary recruits recently inducted during the Magium tournament," the sphere explains. "Several senior officers suspect the recruits enlisted purely to plunder the royal treasury. The guard captain maintains strict personal surveillance over the new arrivals, forbidding them from patrolling unaccompanied. Inciting an internal dispute among the ranks using these suspicions will marginally improve your infiltration window."

"Fascinating," I tell the floating orb. "Any other scenarios where we hold off the guard? More tactical advice?"

"The Overseer protocol possesses no additional data regarding this topic," the sphere responds pleasantly. "Please remember that the Overseer protocol is strictly experimental and may not always yield optimal guidance. As this unit can provide no further operational assistance, you are encouraged to contact an officer of the city watch for further inquiries. We apologize for the inconvenience. Have a pleasant day!"

The propeller whines as the sphere banks sharply and bobs away over the rooftops.

"Well, damn," Hadrik mutters, watching it vanish into the sky. "Do you think any of that was genuine, or are we walking into an elaborate setup?"

"I don't know, but I don't like the look on these bystanders," Daren murmurs. He jerks his chin toward several townspeople who have stopped along the storefronts, watching us with tense, wary eyes. "No telling how much of that exchange carried down the street. We need to clear out before we draw any more attention."

"How do you suppose that scrap metal figured out our business anyway?" Hadrik asks Daren under his breath as we put distance between ourselves and the onlookers.

"They might have tracked us before we even reached the gates," Daren says. "Melindra said surveillance was the original intent, and I haven't been watching the sky since we crossed the threshold."

"If they knew our plans before we walked in, why let us stroll halfway to the castle?" Hadrik asks.

"A trap, perhaps," Daren replies. "Or their network is as broken as the rest of this machinery, and the reports are rotting in an unread dispatch log. Either way, we assume enemies around every turn. Melindra, how far to this plaza?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Pajamas, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Pajamas)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Pajamas
TEXT:

"Not far," Melindra says. "We'll be there in a couple of minutes."

She leads us through two more crooked thoroughfares until the buildings fall back, opening into the wide stone expanse of Perrelion Plaza. The square is packed with hundreds of citizens standing shoulder to shoulder, their murmurs rising in a restless hum as though waiting for a performance.

"Is there some kind of festival scheduled?" I ask in a low voice as we shoulder our way through the edge of the crowd.

"Could be," Daren says, scanning the perimeter. "Or an address from the municipal magistrates. Whatever the reason, it doesn't concern us. Keep your heads down and keep moving."

A shimmer of pale blue light ripples through the air twenty feet above the square, expanding into a colossal magical projection that hangs suspended over the gathering. 

Three figures fill the glowing display. In the center sits a stout man with a heavy gray beard, draped in gold-embroidered velvet and a massive crown—the king of Ollendor. To his right stands a priest in deep blue ceremonial vestments, clutching an ornate scepter crowned with the crest of the God of the Seas. To the left stands an eighteen-year-old boy in loose, pajama-like silk robes, his hands buried deep in his pockets, his eyes closed, and a faint, serene smile resting on his face.

"Damn it," Daren groans under his breath, his knuckles tightening on his shield strap. "Not him again..."

As if tracking the sound across the district, the closed-eyed boy on the screen turns his head directly toward our spot in the square. His lips curl into an unmistakable smirk.

"Did you see that?" Daren whispers, his voice tense. "He looked right at me. How could he hear me over this crowd? Are they somewhere nearby?"

"Be serious," Arraka's voice rasps from the golden amulet at Flower's chest. "He doesn't need to hear your dull little syllables to know what you said. You trot out the exact same complaint every time Eiden steps into view. All he did was track where your heavy aura sits relative to the projection array, catch the spike when you bristled, and time the smirk for when your jaw stopped moving. You'd understand basic resonance if those useless ex-sage tutors of yours had spent five minutes on practical aura drills instead of teaching you how to pick daisies."

Melindra narrows her eyes at the floating projection. "If Eiden sells our location to the king after completely ignoring me at the Thilias arena, I will strangle him with my bare hands."

"He isn't exposing anything," Illuna says, her colder tone cutting through Flower's lips. "Look at the screen. It's a standard state ceremony. The king and Eiden are waiting in silence while that priest chants archaic formulas and batters the stones with his stick. Follow Melindra and ignore the spectacle. These rituals drag on for hours."

"Right, about that," Melindra mutters, looking around the branching colonnades with a faint wince. "The thing is, it's been years since I set foot in this plaza. They've remodeled half the surrounding avenues, and none of the landmarks match what I remember. Just... give me a minute to get my bearings."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Crisis, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Crisis, v_b3_ch2_hearing = 0)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Premonition
TEXT:

After walking a few more blocks, a low, collective hum drifts down from above. Overhead, dozens of brass spheres begin circling in a wide, slow orbit, their shadows flickering across the cobblestones. No matter our pace, the formation maintains a tight perimeter directly over our heads.

"Melindra," Kate says, her eyes locked upward. "Is it standard for the droons to swarm like that?"

"No," Melindra says, her voice tightening. "I've never seen them flock in formation. They're preparing something, but I can't tell what."

"We haven't seen a guard since the gatehouse, and civilian traffic has cleared out entirely," Daren says, his hand resting on the hilt of his blade. "There's an ambush waiting ahead. You think these contraptions are coordinating it?"

"I don't know," Melindra replies. "They had no combat spells when I was here, but eight years is plenty of time for Billy Bob to install something nasty."

#if(v_premonition < 3) {
"They could be relaying our coordinates to the garrison right now," Daren says. "We take them out before they box us in. Kate, clear the sky."

Kate nods, lifting her chin as twenty razor-tipped icicles form in the air above her head, aimed straight at the circling brass eyes.

}
#if(v_premonition >= 3) {
"They could be relaying our coordinates to the garrison right now," Daren says. "We take them out before they box us in. Kate, clear the sky."

Kate nods, lifting her chin as twenty razor-tipped icicles form in the air above her head, aimed straight at the circling brass eyes.

A sudden flash of foresight tears through my thoughts: the brass spheres bank and pivot with uncanny precision, evading the entire volley, then plunge downward with whining rotors to shower us in searing elemental beams.

"Wait! Don't shoot!" I yell, stepping in front of Kate.

The icicles hover in place as Kate turns to me, her brow furrowing. "What is it?"

"A premonition," I say, catching my breath. "The moment you fire, they evade every shard and dive on us with combat spells. If we take them down, we need a coordinated trap, not a single volley."

"Flower and I can ready fire bolts and water lances to strike the instant they evade Kate's initial volley," Illuna says, her colder presence taking hold as Flower's eyes turn bright blue. "However fast their rotors pivot, they won't dodge three staggered waves."

}
choice("Let Kate shoot the metal balls", B3-Ch02b-Contraptions0, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Contraptions0, v_b3_ch2_contraptions = 0) if (v_premonition < 3)
choice(""Maybe Daren should make a barrier for us first, just in case."", B3-Ch02b-Contraptions1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Contraptions1, v_b3_ch2_contraptions = 1) if (v_premonition < 3)
choice(""Yeah, that could work."", B3-Ch02b-Contraptions2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Contraptions2, v_b3_ch2_contraptions = 2) if (v_premonition >= 3)
choice(""Maybe Daren should make a barrier for us first, just in case."", B3-Ch02b-Contraptions3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Contraptions3, v_b3_ch2_contraptions = 3) if (v_premonition >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Contraptions0
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_reflexes == 4 && v_combat_technique < 3 || v_reflexes < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_reflexes == 4 && v_combat_technique >= 3)
#if(v_reflexes < 4) {
Kate unleashes the twenty icicles, driving them straight at the glowing red lenses. The spheres lurch and weave with uncanny precision, the ice slicing harmlessly through empty air. The formation tightens, lenses blazing, and plunges toward us with a chorus of shrill whines, spitting magical beams—crimson fire, crackling lightning, and hissing jets of acid.

Melindra's wind currents lift me into the air as I dodge an ice beam that freezes the cobblestones below. Crossbow in hand, I level the stock at the lightning and acid casters.

Before I can draw a bead, the entire swarm banks sharply in mid-air. Every single brass sphere turns away from the others on the ground and surges straight for me. Airborne priority programming.

I try to drop, but the spheres box me from every angle, rotors screaming as they cut off the descent.

Kate and Flower are already firing, shattering brass shells, but it's too late. A jolt of lightning locks my muscles rigid, and a stream of caustic acid takes my face before I can draw breath.

}
#if(v_reflexes == 4 && v_combat_technique < 3) {
Kate unleashes the twenty icicles, driving them straight at the glowing red lenses. The spheres lurch and weave with uncanny precision, the ice slicing harmlessly through empty air. The formation tightens, lenses blazing, and plunges toward us with a chorus of shrill whines, spitting magical beams—crimson fire, crackling lightning, and hissing jets of acid.

Melindra's wind currents lift me into the air as I dodge an ice beam that freezes the cobblestones below. Crossbow in hand, I level the stock at the lightning and acid casters.

Before I can draw a bead, the entire swarm banks sharply in mid-air. Every single brass sphere turns away from the others on the ground and surges straight for me. Airborne priority programming.

I try to drop, but the spheres box me from every angle, rotors screaming as they cut off the descent.

While Kate and Flower blast spheres out of the sky as fast as they can, I rely entirely on raw reaction speed to twist through the crossfire. But reaction speed only goes so far when the wind currents have a slight delay behind my movements.

I try to shoot back, but the erratic spiraling flight paths defy my aim. A searing beam of fire catches my head, and everything goes black.

}
#if(v_reflexes == 4 && v_combat_technique >= 3) {
Kate unleashes the twenty icicles, driving them straight at the glowing red lenses. The spheres lurch and weave with uncanny precision, the ice slicing harmlessly through empty air. The formation tightens, lenses blazing, and plunges toward us with a chorus of shrill whines, spitting magical beams—crimson fire, crackling lightning, and hissing jets of acid.

Melindra's wind currents lift me into the air as I dodge an ice beam that freezes the cobblestones below. Crossbow in hand, I level the stock at the lightning and acid casters.

Before I can draw a bead, the entire swarm banks sharply in mid-air. Every single brass sphere turns away from the others on the ground and surges straight for me. Airborne priority programming.

I try to drop, but the spheres box me from every angle, rotors screaming as they cut off the descent.

While Kate and Flower blast spheres out of the sky, the swarm closes in. The wind currents lag slightly behind my movements, but my combat training compensates: reading the angle of the lens, anticipating the rotor tilt, baiting a turn before slipping beneath it.

I loose two bolts in rapid succession, punching straight through two glowing apertures, buying the critical seconds needed for Kate's icicles and Illuna's water lances to shred the remaining droons into tumbling scrap.

Daren lowers his shield, looking at the smoking brass wreckage littering the street. "That's the end of those droons, then."

"Only the ones overhead," Melindra says. "The city is crawling with them."

"Out of sight, out of mind," Daren says. "I'm more concerned with the garrison ahead. I can feel their auras shifting to choke off the side alleys. Breaking through is going to be messy."

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_reflexes == 4 && v_combat_technique < 3 || v_reflexes < 4)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_reflexes == 4 && v_combat_technique < 3 || v_reflexes < 4)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_reflexes == 4 && v_combat_technique < 3 || v_reflexes < 4)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Ambush, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Ambush) if (v_reflexes == 4 && v_combat_technique >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Contraptions1
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_reflexes < 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_reflexes >= 3)
#if(v_reflexes < 3) {
"Maybe Daren should make a barrier for us first, just in case."

"Good thinking," Daren says. "Those contraptions might retaliate the moment we draw magic. In close, all of you."

We pull tight around Daren as he raises his shield, expanding a translucent dome of protective white light overhead. Safe behind the ward, Kate unleashes her icicles at the hovering eyes. The spheres tilt and dodge cleanly, then dive upon the barrier, unloading a relentless barrage of elemental fire, lightning, and acid.

"Damn it..." Daren grunts, boots sliding back across the cobblestones. "The ward hasn't fully regenerated after the battle with the large gate golems. It won't hold under this constant hammering. Destroy them now!"

As the barrier shudders under a dozen simultaneous spells, hairline fractures spiderwebbing across the glowing dome, Kate launches a second wave of ice while Illuna and Flower fire intersecting arcs of flame and high-pressure water. Half the brass spheres shatter into scrap, but the fractures across Daren's barrier widen with a deafening crystalline screech.

"The barrier's about to break!" Daren shouts. "Be ready!"

As the dome shatters in a shower of white sparks, we break in different directions. Several droons lock onto me as I rise on Melindra's wind currents, and my ascent only draws the rest of the surviving swarm straight after me.

I twist desperately in the air, but the barrage is too dense. Before the others can reach me, two intersecting fire beams converge on my chest, killing me instantly.

}
#if(v_reflexes >= 3) {
"Maybe Daren should make a barrier for us first, just in case."

"Good thinking," Daren says. "Those contraptions might retaliate the moment we draw magic. In close, all of you."

We pull tight around Daren as he raises his shield, expanding a translucent dome of protective white light overhead. Safe behind the ward, Kate unleashes her icicles at the hovering eyes. The spheres tilt and dodge cleanly, then dive upon the barrier, unloading a relentless barrage of elemental fire, lightning, and acid.

"Damn it..." Daren grunts, boots sliding back across the cobblestones. "The ward hasn't fully regenerated after the battle with the large gate golems. It won't hold under this constant hammering. Destroy them now!"

As the barrier shudders under a dozen simultaneous spells, hairline fractures spiderwebbing across the glowing dome, Kate launches a second wave of ice while Illuna and Flower fire intersecting arcs of flame and high-pressure water. Half the brass spheres shatter into scrap, but the fractures across Daren's barrier widen with a deafening crystalline screech.

"The barrier's about to break!" Daren shouts. "Be ready!"

As the dome shatters in a shower of white sparks, we break in different directions. Several droons lock onto me as I rise on Melindra's wind currents, and my ascent only draws the rest of the surviving swarm straight after me.

My reflexes kick in, ducking and rolling through the air currents just inches ahead of the scorching beams. I buy enough time for Kate, Illuna, and Flower to obliterate the spheres chasing me, and with a few well-aimed crossbow bolts, we finish off the stragglers.

"That's the end of those droons, then," Daren says, looking at the smoking brass wreckage littering the street.

"Only the ones overhead," Melindra says. "The city is crawling with them."

"Out of sight, out of mind," Daren says. "I'm more concerned with the garrison ahead. I can feel their auras shifting to choke off the side alleys. Breaking through is going to be messy."

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Ambush, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Ambush) if (v_reflexes >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Contraptions2
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_reflexes < 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_reflexes >= 3)
#if(v_reflexes < 3) {
"Yeah, that could work."

"Let's do it, then," Illuna says, her hands sparking with pressurized water while Flower channels twin flames.

Kate looses her icicles first, baiting the spheres into an evasive tilt. The instant they bank, Flower's fire arrows and Illuna's water lances strike the opening. Even with the coordinated trap, half the swarm slips through the crossfire and wheels around, diving straight for me as I take to the air.

I try to evade, but the air currents lag behind the incoming fire. Before the others can reach me, two intersecting fire beams converge on my chest, killing me instantly.

}
#if(v_reflexes >= 3) {
"Yeah, that could work."

"Let's do it, then," Illuna says, her hands sparking with pressurized water while Flower channels twin flames.

Kate looses her icicles first, baiting the spheres into an evasive tilt. The instant they bank, Flower's fire arrows and Illuna's water lances strike the opening. Even with the coordinated trap, half the swarm slips through the crossfire and wheels around, diving straight for me as I take to the air.

My reflexes keep me ducking and rolling through the air currents just inches ahead of the scorching beams. I buy enough time for Kate, Illuna, and Flower to obliterate the spheres chasing me, and with a few well-aimed crossbow bolts, we finish off the stragglers.

"That's the end of those droons, then," Daren says, looking at the smoking brass wreckage littering the street.

"Only the ones overhead," Melindra says. "The city is crawling with them."

"Out of sight, out of mind," Daren says. "Frankly, I'm more worried about the guards that are trying to set up an ambush ahead of us. From what I'm sensing, it seems that they're trying to block off our other escape routes as well. Shaking them off is going to be a little tricky."

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_reflexes < 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Ambush, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Ambush) if (v_reflexes >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Contraptions3
TEXT:

"Maybe Daren should make a barrier for us first, just in case."

"Good thinking," Daren says. "Those contraptions might retaliate the moment we draw magic. In close, all of you."

We pull tight around Daren as he raises his shield, expanding a translucent dome of protective white light overhead. Safe behind the ward, Illuna and Flower ready their spells as Kate conjures her icicles.

Kate looses her ice first, baiting the spheres into an evasive tilt. The instant they bank, Flower and Illuna unleash their volley. Half the droons shatter instantly, but the survivors plunge down, hammering Daren's barrier with fire, lightning, and acid.

"Damn it..." Daren grunts, his boots sliding back as the barrier groans. "The ward hasn't fully regenerated after the gate golems. It won't hold under this constant hammering. Finish this quickly!"

As the barrier shudders under the concentrated barrage, Kate sends a second wave of ice while Illuna and Flower tear through the sky with water lances and fire arrows. The three of them obliterate the remaining droons just as fractures spread across the entire surface of the dome.

Daren lowers his shield, letting the cracked barrier dissolve into white sparks. "That's the end of those droons, then."

"Only the ones overhead," Melindra says. "The city is crawling with them."

"Out of sight, out of mind," Daren says. "Frankly, I'm more worried about the guards that are trying to set up an ambush ahead of us. From what I'm sensing, it seems that they're trying to block off our other escape routes as well. Shaking them off is going to be a little tricky."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Ambush, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Ambush)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Ambush
TEXT:

"Well, well," Arraka's voice rasps from the golden amulet. "Look who's finally using magical sense to spot an ambush. Maybe those aura training sessions weren't a complete waste of time after all."

"Some of our escape routes only have a pair of sentries posted," Melindra says. "We can overpower them in seconds."

"And they'll shout into their transceivers and bring the whole garrison down on our heads," Daren counters.

"Better than walking into a crossfire," Melindra says. "We invaded their city. Resistance comes with the territory."

"Fair enough," Daren says. "The most direct route that avoids the main cordon. Lead on."

"This way," Melindra says, slipping down a narrow, deserted alley. "Follow me."

Two sentries stand at the far end of the lane, one already reaching for a brass transceiver at his hip. We drop them before a single distress call goes out, stepping over them into the cross-street.

The quiet doesn't last. Heavy boots pound the stone behind us as the primary ambush force rounds the corner, shouting orders as they close the distance.

"What now?" Hadrik asks, cracking his knuckles. "Turn and smash?"

"No," Daren says. "We buy time. Flower, drop a fire wall across the full width of this street. Force them to double back."

"On it," Flower says, her hands sweeping out as a roaring wall of crimson flames erupts across the avenue, cutting off the pursuit.

"Keep moving!" Daren urges.

"Another squad approaching from the side street!" Melindra calls out, pointing down an intersecting lane.

Before anyone can respond, Kate strides to the corner, extends her palm, and looses a freezing beam across the stones. The entire alleyway glazes over in thick, mirror-smooth ice.

"There," Kate says, stepping back into formation. "Frozen solid. Unless they brought skates, they'll be sliding on their backs. Shall we move?"

"Perrelion Plaza is right ahead," Melindra says. "Once we're in the square, we'll pick our approach to the palace."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Holographic, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Holographic)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Holographic
TEXT:

She leads us through two more crooked thoroughfares until the buildings fall back, opening into the wide stone expanse of Perrelion Plaza. The square is packed with hundreds of citizens standing shoulder to shoulder, their murmurs rising in a restless hum as though waiting for a performance.

"Is there some kind of festival scheduled?" I ask in a low voice as we shoulder our way through the edge of the crowd.

"Could be," Daren says, scanning the perimeter. "Or an address from the municipal magistrates. Whatever the reason, it doesn't concern us. Keep your heads down and keep moving."

A shimmer of pale blue light ripples through the air twenty feet above the square, expanding into a colossal magical projection that hangs suspended over the gathering.

Three figures fill the glowing display. In the center sits a stout man with a heavy gray beard, draped in gold-embroidered velvet and a massive crown—the king of Ollendor. To his right stands a priest in deep blue ceremonial vestments, clutching an ornate scepter crowned with the crest of the God of the Seas. To the left stands an eighteen-year-old boy in loose, pajama-like silk robes, his hands buried deep in his pockets, his eyes closed, and a faint, serene smile resting on his face.

"Damn it," Daren groans under his breath, his knuckles tightening on his shield strap. "Not him again..."

As if tracking the sound across the district, the closed-eyed boy on the screen turns his head directly toward our spot in the square. His lips curl into an unmistakable smirk.

"Did you see that?" Daren whispers, his voice tense. "He looked right at me. How could he hear me over this crowd? Are they somewhere nearby?"

"Be serious," Arraka's voice rasps from the golden amulet at Flower's chest. "He doesn't need to hear your dull little syllables to know what you said. You trot out the exact same complaint every time Eiden steps into view. All he did was track where your heavy aura sits relative to the projection array, catch the spike when you bristled, and time the smirk for when your jaw stopped moving. You'd understand basic resonance if those useless ex-sage tutors of yours had spent five minutes on practical aura drills instead of teaching you how to pick daisies."

Melindra narrows her eyes at the floating projection. "If Eiden sells our location to the king after completely ignoring me at the Thilias arena, I will strangle him with my bare hands."

"He isn't exposing anything," Illuna says, her colder tone cutting through Flower's lips. "Look at the screen. It's a standard state ceremony. The king and Eiden are waiting in silence while that priest chants archaic formulas and batters the stones with his stick. Follow Melindra and ignore the spectacle. These rituals drag on for hours."

"Right, about that," Melindra mutters, looking around the branching colonnades with a faint wince. "The thing is, it's been years since I set foot in this plaza. They've remodeled half the surrounding avenues, and none of the landmarks match what I remember. Just... give me a minute to get my bearings."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Crisis, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Crisis, v_b3_ch2_hearing = 0)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Crisis
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3 || v_b3_ch2_goblins == 4 || v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3) {
"At least we've got some quality entertainment while we wait," Hadrik says, grinning with dry sarcasm. "What do you reckon this grand ritual is for?"

"We'll find out shortly," Kate says. "The priest is nearly finished with his preparations."

The man in blue robes strikes the stone platform twice more with the base of his scepter, then steps back, bowing his head as the king of Ollendor waddles forward to the center of the display.

"My beloved people!" the king proclaims, his voice booming over the square. "As announced at yesterday's dawn, our Creator has finally returned to us. I spoke with him in private yesterday, and he has graciously agreed to participate in this ceremony to receive the divine blessing of the God of the Seas, just like every true citizen of Ollendor. Once he is woven into our city's soul, our prosperity will know no bounds, and together with him we shall never need to fear any enemy again!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins != 3 && v_b3_ch2_goblins != 4 && v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 3) {
As Melindra concentrates on the branching colonnades, the gentle air currents lifting my boots fade away.

"At least we don't have to worry about an ambush here with all these townspeople crowded around," Hadrik says, glancing across the square. "What do you reckon this grand ritual is for?"

"We'll find out shortly," Kate says. "The priest is nearly finished with his preparations."

The man in blue robes strikes the stone platform twice more with the base of his scepter, then steps back, bowing his head as the king of Ollendor waddles forward to the center of the display.

"My beloved people!" the king proclaims, his voice booming over the square. "As announced at yesterday's dawn, our Creator has finally returned to us. I spoke with him in private yesterday, and he has graciously agreed to participate in this ceremony to receive the divine blessing of the God of the Seas, just like every true citizen of Ollendor. Once he is woven into our city's soul, our prosperity will know no bounds, and together with him we shall never need to fear any enemy again!"

}
"Actually, oh great and wise king," Eiden's voice floats across the square, light and melodic, "my role for now remains purely that of an observer, rather than an active participant in your municipal politics. I have reason to believe your kingdom will face a rather severe trial in the very near future, and I am deeply curious to see how you and your people handle the crisis. I have heard glowing praise regarding your skilled leadership, and I look forward to observing your decision-making up close. I trust you will not disappoint my expectations."

"Of course, Creator!" the king puffs his chest, beaming with pride. "Whatever this crisis may be, we shall prove our strength beyond measure. High priest, commence the ceremony!"

The priest resumes his rhythmic chanting, striking the scepter against the stones once more as both the king and Eiden fall silent.

"I knew it," Daren growls under his breath. "That bastard knows exactly what we came to do, and he pulled up a front-row seat for the spectacle. He'll sit there sipping tea while the rest of us slaughter each other for his amusement."

"He can drink all the tea on the continent as long as he keeps his hands in his pockets," Melindra says. "Let's just hope he doesn't grow bored halfway through and decide to join in the fun."

choice(""I think he just wants to see how things will play out. I doubt he'll join in."", B3-Ch02b-Doubt, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Doubt)
choice(""Even if there is interference from him, I don't think it will be anything too major."", B3-Ch02b-Major, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Major)
choice(""You think he might want to fight us?"", B3-Ch02b-Fight, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Fight)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Doubt
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch2_hearing,0) if (v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2 || v_hearing < 3)
set(v_b3_ch2_hearing,1) if (v_hearing >= 3 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2)
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2 || v_hearing < 3) {
"I think he just wants to see how things will play out. I doubt he'll join in."

"A bit optimistic, but we'll find out soon enough," Melindra says. "Anyway, forget Eiden for now. I've found our turn. Over here."

We follow Melindra toward the eastern edge of the plaza, where the press of the crowd thins out against a line of shuttered stone buildings.

}
#if(v_hearing >= 3 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2) {
"I think he just wants to see how things will play out. I doubt he'll join in."

"A bit optimistic, but we'll find out soon enough," Melindra says. "Anyway, forget Eiden for now. I've found our turn. Over here."

We follow Melindra toward the eastern edge of the plaza, where the press of the crowd thins out against a line of shuttered stone buildings.

As we move clear of the square, muffled voices arguing in an adjacent alley catch my ear.

"The ceremony's already underway," a deep, gravelly voice rumbles. "Quit your whining. Arriving a couple minutes late isn't going to collapse the kingdom."

"Are you forgetting who dragged us into the market stalls to begin with?" a sharp, older voice snaps back.

"Nobody forced you to tail me while I bought rations," the gravelly voice grunts. "You could have marched ahead on your own."

"And leave you unsupervised for ten seconds?" the older man retorts. "You might have fooled the recruiting officers, but I know what you are. As long as you wear this uniform, you answer to my command. Do you understand, recruit Gondrok?"

"Whatever you say, captain," the first voice replies with a low, dismissive chuckle, and the alley falls silent.

}
Near the margin of the square, Melindra suddenly raises a fist, halting us in our tracks. Her eyes lock onto the mouth of the alley ahead. Three men in matching royal-blue tunics over dark steel cuirasses step into the sunlight. None of them carry the protective ward glow that marks untouchable locals, except the eldest.

The first tournament recruit is towering and thick-shouldered, built like a fortress—reminiscent of Bruce from the arena, but with a shaved bald scalp, a dense black beard reaching his chest, and cold, battle-hardened eyes. He carries no weapons on his belt, knuckles scarred from years of bare-handed brawling.

The second recruit is slender and sharp-featured, his skin a smooth golden-brown, a few shades lighter than Daren's. Short black hair frames an alert face in his early thirties, and twin curved longswords hang from his sash in paired scabbards.

The senior officer leading them is an elderly man in his sixties, his white hair tied in a tight queue, his weathered face creased with deep lines. His blue tunic bears silver braid and gold insignia across the shoulders. Like the massive brawler beside him, he carries no steel at his hip, relying entirely on spellcraft.

As the three guards clear the alley, their eyes lock onto our group. They adjust their course, stepping across the stones directly toward us.

"Royal guards," Melindra whispers, stepping half a pace back. "Careful. Their auras are clamped down tight. I can't read their reserve."

"Well, well. If it isn't my good old friend Hadrik!" the massive brawler says, a broad smirk spreading across his face. "I had a feeling you'd find your way into this tournament."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Gontrok, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Gontrok)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Major
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch2_hearing,0) if (v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2 || v_hearing < 3)
set(v_b3_ch2_hearing,1) if (v_hearing >= 3 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2)
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2 || v_hearing < 3) {
"Even if there is interference from him, I don't think it will be anything too major."

"That's my guess too," Melindra says. "If he does anything, it'll be dropping cryptic hints to the king's court to keep the game alive, nothing direct. Anyway, forget Eiden for now. I've found our turn. Over here."

We follow Melindra toward the eastern edge of the plaza, where the press of the crowd thins out against a line of shuttered stone buildings.

}
