}
#if(v_hearing >= 3 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2) {
"You think he might want to fight us?"

"No, that's not what I meant," Melindra says. "So long as I'm traveling with this party, he won't strike us. But I wouldn't put it past him to feed the king advice once the fighting starts, just to keep from falling asleep. Anyway, forget Eiden for now. I've found our turn. Over here."

We follow Melindra toward the eastern edge of the plaza, where the press of the crowd thins out against a line of shuttered stone buildings.

As we move clear of the square, muffled voices arguing in an adjacent alley catch my ear.

"The ceremony's already underway," a deep, gravelly voice rumbles. "Quit your whining. Arriving a couple minutes late isn't going to collapse the kingdom."

"Are you forgetting who dragged us into the market stalls to begin with?" a sharp, older voice snaps back.

"Nobody forced you to tail me while I bought rations," the gravelly voice grunts. "You could have marched ahead on your own."

"And leave you unsupervised for ten seconds?" the older man retorts. "You might have fooled the recruiting officers, but I know what you are. As long as you wear this uniform, you answer to my command. Do you understand, recruit Gondrok?"

"Whatever you say, captain," the first voice replies with a low, dismissive chuckle, and the alley falls silent.

Near the margin of the square, Melindra suddenly raises a fist, halting us in our tracks. Her eyes lock onto the mouth of the alley ahead. Three men in matching royal-blue tunics over dark steel cuirasses step into the sunlight. None of them carry the protective ward glow that marks untouchable locals, except the eldest.

The first tournament recruit is towering and thick-shouldered, built like a fortress—reminiscent of Bruce from the arena, but with a shaved bald scalp, a dense black beard reaching his chest, and cold, battle-hardened eyes. He carries no weapons on his belt, knuckles scarred from years of bare-handed brawling.

The second recruit is slender and sharp-featured, his skin a smooth golden-brown, a few shades lighter than Daren's. Short black hair frames an alert face in his early thirties, and twin curved longswords hang from his sash in paired scabbards.

}
The senior officer leading them is an elderly man in his sixties, his white hair tied in a tight queue, his weathered face creased with deep lines. His blue tunic bears silver braid and gold insignia across the shoulders. Like the massive brawler beside him, he carries no steel at his hip, relying entirely on spellcraft.

As the three guards clear the alley, their eyes lock onto our group. They adjust their course, stepping across the stones directly toward us.

"Royal guards," Melindra whispers, stepping half a pace back. "Careful. Their auras are clamped down tight. I can't read their reserve."

"Well, well. If it isn't my good old friend Hadrik!" the massive brawler says, a broad smirk spreading across his face. "I had a feeling you'd find your way into this tournament."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Gontrok, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Gontrok)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Gontrok
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch2_hearing == 1) {
"Gontrok..." Hadrik's voice drops, a dark, dangerous edge hardening behind his eyes.

"You don't look thrilled to see me, Hadrik," Gontrok says, his broad chest rumbling with amusement. "What's the matter? Still nursing that ancient grudge over the dwarf village? Then again, you wouldn't be Hadrik if you didn't cling to every petty dispute for a hundred years."

Gontrok roars with laughter. Hadrik's right fist closes, slow and deliberate, and the aura around his knuckles draws in so dense that the air above the cobblestones distorts like heat over a forge.

"You know this dwarf, recruit?" the captain asks, his tone sharp.

"He's no dwarf, captain," Gontrok says. "He's a northern giant, same as me. The tournament organizers likely ordered him into dwarf form so he wouldn't terrify the locals, just like they made me take this human skin. Any giant traveling south has to learn compression shapeshifting or the civilized kingdoms slam their gates in our faces."

The voices click. These two are the captain and recruit from the alley argument. If an opening presents itself, that friction might be the only lever we have.

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_hearing == 0) {
"Gontrok..." Hadrik's voice drops, a dark, dangerous edge hardening behind his eyes.

"You don't look thrilled to see me, Hadrik," Gontrok says, his broad chest rumbling with amusement. "What's the matter? Still nursing that ancient grudge over the dwarf village? Then again, you wouldn't be Hadrik if you didn't cling to every petty dispute for a hundred years."

Gontrok roars with laughter. Hadrik's right fist closes, slow and deliberate, and the aura around his knuckles draws in so dense that the air above the cobblestones distorts like heat over a forge.

"You know this dwarf, recruit?" the captain asks, his tone sharp.

"He's no dwarf, captain," Gontrok says. "He's a northern giant, same as me. The tournament organizers likely ordered him into dwarf form so he wouldn't terrify the locals, just like they made me take this human skin. Any giant traveling south has to learn compression shapeshifting or the civilized kingdoms slam their gates in our faces."

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 3 || v_b3_ch2_goblins == 4 || v_b3_ch2_infiltration == 3) {
"I care nothing for giant customs, recruit," the captain says, his voice ice-cold. "What I demand to know is who authorized these outsiders past our gates. Name the citizen of Ollendor who supplied your party with the password."

"We cannot disclose his name," Daren says firmly. "We gave our word."

"No secrets exist from the royal guard," the captain replies. "I ask one final time: who invited you?"

Gontrok chuckles. "You know as well as I do nobody invited them, captain. They eavesdropped on a drunken farmer or bribed an outcast, then fed the sentries the same rehearsed lie."

"If you refuse to identify your sponsor, you will be taken into custody," the captain tells us.

"And if we decline?" Daren asks.

"Summary execution," the captain says.

"Now that's the order I've been waiting for!" Gontrok says, cracking his knuckles. "I've been itching to test Hadrik again for decades, and the gods won't offer a cleaner chance. Stand back, both of you! I'll break the lot of them myself!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins != 3 && v_b3_ch2_goblins != 4 && v_b3_ch2_infiltration != 3) {
"I care nothing for giant customs, recruit," the captain says, his voice ice-cold. "What I care about is why these criminals are walking our plaza unmolested. By the dispatch reports, this is the very band that smashed our gatehouse and breached the perimeter."

"Then we execute them here and save the garrison the paperwork, captain," Gontrok says, cracking his knuckles. "I've been itching to test Hadrik again for decades, and the gods won't offer a cleaner chance. Stand back, both of you! I'll break the lot of them myself!"

}
"Even if you hadn't ordered me back, I wouldn't draw steel," the golden-brown royal guard says. "His Majesty stationed us to secure the plaza during the ceremony, not pick street brawls with tournament travelers. If they broke municipal law, leave them to the watch. Why dirty our blades?"

"Insubordination!" the captain snaps. "I command you to engage as a unit, or I'll see you in irons by sundown!"

"Zip it, old man," Gontrok says. "I take orders from the king, not a paranoid captain. File a complaint if my manners offend you. In the meantime, I'm taking my old friend apart."

Hadrik's grin returns, tight and humorless. "You reckon you can take me alone?"

"You forget, Hadrik—I spent years dissecting every flaw in your form back when we sparred across the northern peaks," Gontrok says. "You learned my habits too, but I've spent the decades since rebuilding my style from the bone up. Tell me... did you ever bother to change yours?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Gontrok2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Gontrok2)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Gontrok2
TEXT:

Gontrok digs his boots into the stone. The cobblestones explode in a spiderweb of crushed granite as he launches across the square, closing thirty paces in the blink of an eye. Hadrik leaps backward on instinct, but Gontrok is already inside his guard, driving a heavy left hook toward the ribs. Hadrik drops his elbow to absorb the strike—a feint. Gontrok's right hand snaps upward, iron fingers locking around Hadrik's skull, and slams his face into the pavement with ground-shaking violence. The impact gouges a crater into the street, rock shards whistling through the air like shrapnel.

Without pausing for Hadrik to roll, Gontrok heaves him aloft by the hair, waits for the apex, and catches the dwarf mid-descent with a brutal side kick. The concussive blow launches Hadrik straight through the brick facade of the nearest building, tearing through wall after wall in a deafening cascade of collapsing masonry until he vanishes deep within the city blocks. Screaming civilians scatter in all directions, stampeding away from the plaza.

Across the stones, Kate unleashes nearly a hundred jagged icicles at the guard captain. The old man doesn't flinch. A shimmer passes through the air, and the entire crystalline volley halts dead in mid-flight inches from his chest, flips in reverse, and rockets back toward Kate with blistering velocity. Kate throws up an ice wall just in time to shatter the reflected shards, but the captain thrusts his open palm forward. An invisible, crushing weight slams downward, driving Kate to her knees and pinning her flat against the stones—the same crushing aura manipulation Eiden and the God of Time used during our trials.

Flower ignites her fire jets, leaping upward to break the line of sight, but the unseen force crashes down on her shoulders, smashing her into the dirt beside Kate. Before the captain can close his fist, Leila blurs into motion, twin daggers drawn, forcing the old mage into desperate close-quarters melee. Lacking the footwork to evade her strikes, the captain is forced to divert his invisible bursts to deflect her blades, loosening his grip on the suppression field enough for Kate and Flower to push themselves onto their elbows.

Through it all, the dark-skinned guard leans casually against a shop pillar, arms crossed, watching the brawl with cold indifference. Gontrok turns slowly toward Daren and me, knuckles cracking. Hadrik hasn't reemerged from the rubble.

"Stay behind me, Barry," Daren says, raising his shield.

"Well now!" Gontrok's grin widens. "The healer in armor. I've heard the eastern ballads. Let's see how those songs hold up against real northern iron."

He lunges, driving a thunderous punch at Daren's head. Daren sways aside on practiced footwork, letting the massive fist split empty air, and counters with a swift diagonal slash of his enchanted blade. But Gontrok's reflexes are razor-sharp; the giant kicks off his back foot, somersaulting backward just clear of the steel.

"Not bad for a priest!" Gontrok laughs as Daren presses forward. "Takes more than quick hands to fell a giant, though!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch02b-Gontrok3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch02b-Gontrok3)


ID: B3-Ch02b-Gontrok3
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 0) {
Daren closes the distance, blade flashing. Gontrok slips the thrust and pivots into a sweeping spin-kick. Daren brings his shield up, but the sheer kinetic mass tears through his stance, launching him violently into the stone wall behind us. Before Daren can push off the ground, Gontrok is on him, driving a heavy boot straight into his breastplate. The metal caves with a sickening crunch, Daren's back smashes the masonry, and his enchanted sword slips from his numb fingers, clattering onto the dirt.

"You won't be needing that where you're headed," Gontrok says, kicking the sword across the cobblestones.

Panic seizes my chest as I watch Daren slump against the cracked brickwork. He can't match this strength. Hadrik is buried ten houses away. Leila, Kate, and Flower are locked in a desperate struggle with the captain. I'm standing alone before a giant.

Neither my dagger nor my crossbow bolts can pierce giant hide. There has to be an angle. A loophole. Think, Barry. Why won't the shaking stop?!

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar != 0) {
Daren closes the distance, blade flashing. Gontrok slips the thrust and pivots into a sweeping spin-kick. Daren brings his shield up, but the sheer kinetic mass tears through his stance, launching him violently into the stone wall behind us. Before Daren can push off the ground, Gontrok is on him, driving a heavy boot straight into his breastplate. The metal caves with a sickening crunch, Daren's back smashes the masonry, and his enchanted sword slips from his numb fingers, clattering onto the dirt.

"You won't be needing that where you're headed," Gontrok says, kicking the sword across the cobblestones.

Panic seizes my chest as I watch Daren slump against the cracked brickwork. He can't match this strength. Hadrik is buried ten houses away. Leila, Kate, and Flower are locked in a desperate struggle with the captain. I'm standing alone before a giant.

Neither my scimitar nor my crossbow bolts can pierce giant hide. There has to be an angle. A loophole. Think, Barry. Why won't the shaking stop?!

}
As cold sweat breaks across my neck, gentle currents of air begin swirling around my boots and shoulders. The soft breeze brushes against my skin, carrying an eerie, grounding stillness that slows my racing pulse and steadies my hands. Melindra's flight spell. I look back; across the square, Melindra meets my eyes, her face dead serious, and gives a sharp nod toward Daren, who is clutching his cracked ribs as Gontrok towers over him.

She's telling me to intervene. But how?

My gaze drops to the cobblestones ten paces away. Daren's enchanted sword lies in the dust. A weapon forged to cut through magical protections. If I can reach it—and if I can land a strike...

#if(v_b3_ch2_confess == 1) {
The Overseer's analysis flashes through my memory. The green-eyed drone mentioned the captain suspects the new tournament recruits of plotting to loot the treasury. It even suggested exploiting the rift. What happens if I pour fuel on the fire?

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_hearing == 1 && v_b3_ch2_confess != 1) {
The alley argument echoes in my mind. The captain and Gontrok were already at each other's throats over trust and authority. What happens if I pour fuel on the fire?

}
